- Can't Stop probabilities, strategy simulations, and design balance (source: 03_cant_stop_addicted_to_shindig/source.txt)
    Summary: Can't Stop analysis shows four-dice odds flatten the distribution, middle columns dominate, probabilistic stopping wins, and forced moves balance design. (¶1, ¶2, ¶12, ¶22, ¶28)
        - 1 Push-your-luck rules: roll four dice, pair freely, bust loses turn progress. (¶3, ¶4)
            - 1.1 Evidence: Board columns 2 to 12 vary 3 to 13 steps; complete three to win. (¶4)
            - 1.2 Forced-move rule requires taking every legal move from the chosen pairing. (¶5)
            - 1.3 Example: Chasing 7 on {6,7,8} can force an unwanted 2 onto the board. (¶6)
        - 2 Four-dice pairing flattens the probability distribution versus two dice. (¶7, ¶8, ¶9)
            - 2.1 Evidence: Two-dice baseline: 7 hits 16.67%, six times more likely than 2. (¶7)
            - 2.2 Evidence: Four dice yield exactly three possible pairings per roll. (¶9)
            - 2.3 Evidence: Making a 2 needs two 1s: 171/1296, about 13.2%. (¶10)
            - 2.4 Evidence: Making a 7 via inclusion-exclusion: 834/1296, about 64.4%. (¶11)
            - 2.5 Nuance: Probability ratio 4.88 nearly matches length ratio 4.33; designers thought carefully. (¶12)
        - 3 Column combinations matter more than individual column odds. (¶13, ¶14, ¶15)
            - 3.1 Evidence: Expected rolls cluster near 20; long column 7 completes slightly faster than 2. (¶13, ¶14)
            - 3.2 Evidence: {6,7,8} succeeds 92% per roll; {2,3,12} only 43.8%. (¶15)
            - 3.3 Evidence: 37 combos rate excellent; median succeeds 79.6%, worst collapse to 43.8%. (¶16)
        - 4 Stopping rule weighs expected gain against bust-weighted unsaved progress. (¶17, ¶18, ¶19)
            - 4.1 Evidence: Roll on {6,7,8} with five steps banked; stop on {2,3,12} with two. (¶17)
            - 4.2 Nuance: Only 39.8% of {6,7,8} rolls are clean; {2,3,12} just 2.3%. (¶18)
            - 4.3 Concession: Continuing current columns usually beats switching, but author hedges: switching unanalyzed quantitatively. (¶19)
        - 5 3.6M-game simulations crown probabilistic, consistent strategies over fast ones. (¶20, ¶22)
            - 5.1 Evidence: 38 strategy families tested across single-player and head-to-head simulations. (¶20)
            - 5.2 Evidence: GreedyUntil1Col fastest at 10.5 turns but busts 7.5 times per game. (¶21)
            - 5.3 Evidence: FiftyPercentSurvival wins 69.84% by stopping below 50% cumulative survival. (¶22)
            - 5.4 Nuance: Consistency beats raw speed; pure greedy loses nearly everything. (¶23, ¶25)
            - 5.5 Evidence: First player wins 55.59%, an 11.18% edge exceeding chess and Go. (¶24)
        - 6 Board is nearly balanced singly; forced moves compensate combination imbalance. (¶26, ¶27, ¶28)
            - 6.1 Evidence: Single-column completion deviates at most 13.6% from 20 rolls. (¶26)
            - 6.2 Evidence: Adding one step per middle column would quadruple balance precision. (¶27)
            - 6.3 Nuance: Forced moves contaminate strong combos, keeping strategy choices interesting. (¶28)
        - 7 Play middle columns consistently; designers should favor playability over perfection. (¶29, ¶30)
            - 7.1 Evidence: Aim for columns 5-9, avoid edge columns, prioritize consistency over aggression. (¶29)
            - 7.2 Evidence: All code, simulations, and datasets are published for reuse. (¶30)
    - Cross-links
        - 1.2 qualifies 3.2 (¶6, ¶18) note: forced moves contaminate even the best column combos
        - 2.5 supports 6.1 (¶12, ¶26) note: probability-length match underpins balance verdict
        - 4.2 supports 6.3 (¶18, ¶28) note: clean-move scarcity evidences the compensating rule
        - 5.4 supports 7.1 (¶23, ¶29) note: consistency finding grounds the player advice
